0.58.5 (2026-08-16)
- Moved the new Dogtown people out of the overworld and into interiors opened
  from the supply, doc and garage delivery spots after each delivery is found.
- Replaced placeholder people sprites with compact 34x34 2bpp sprites converted
  from the supplied character sheets for Kit, Mara, Vera, Ada and Bram.
- DATA is now a browsable People Index with locked entries and sprite portraits;
  quest status moved under the map on the MAP tab.
- Swapped pause-menu wheel rotation only: left wheel moves rows, right wheel
  changes tabs, and left click still selects.
- Split the Solomon Ray final fight into its own small module.
- Rotjaw's track and tavern dialogue now ties the motorcycle to Ray's road
  blockade and Dogtown's missing deliveries.
- DATA now labels the list as People of Interest and uses plainer NPC notes.
- Temporary DEBUG BOSS title-menu entry launches SOLOMON.JS directly; the real
  hotel office boss also uses that smaller sprite-only fight module.
- Solomon's final battle now uses Pokemon-style command flow: FIRE, SCORCH,
  CHUCK and ITEMS. ITEMS opens STIM/PSYCHO/BACK, and Chuck is a real tactical
  attack that can draw Ray's next shot off target.
- Converted the supplied office background and animated player, Chuck and
  Solomon battle sheets into compact 2bpp battle assets. The final fight now
  draws the office background plus lazy-loaded action poses.
- On-device JavaScript is 129,667 bytes after the final-battle art wiring.

0.58.4 (2026-08-16)
- Added three talkable Dogtown locals to unused house spots on the Dogtown map.
- Added two talkable patrons inside Rot's Tavern.
- Pause DATA now includes a People Met index: known names unlock after talking,
  while unmet Dogtown/Tavern people stay listed as LOCKED.
- On-device JavaScript is 119,024 bytes after the Dogtown people index.

0.58.3 (2026-08-16)
- Converted the new player, Chuck and Solomon Ray sprite sheets into compact
  transparent 2bpp final-battle sprites.
- Solomon's office fight now draws the sheet sprites for idle, attack and down
  poses, while keeping the old line-drawn figures as missing-asset fallbacks.
- On-device JavaScript is 116,136 bytes after the final-battle sprite wiring.

0.58.2 (2026-08-16)
- Merged the updated HAMCo/Solomon Ray story from BIGIRON_FULL_LOW_MEMORY_FIXED.zip.
  The Big Iron is now a HAMCo service revolver, Scorched rounds are test loads,
  and Solomon Ray is the office boss whose terminal completes the reveal.
- Converted BIGIRONLOB.png into the lobby's 480x296 2bpp HR0.BIN background.
- Rebuilt main-lobby collision and interaction points for the new art: bottom
  entrance, front desk, stairs, elevator, chairs, tables and side machinery.
- On-device JavaScript is 115,470 bytes after the story and lobby rebuild.

0.58.1 (2026-08-15)
- Every identifier inside the modules renamed to readable words; source moved to src/.
- Shipped scripts/ are generated from src/ by tools/build.mjs (terser, mangle and strip only;
  no compression passes, because Espruino does not block-scope let/const).
- Each readable and each shipped module is proved to be an alpha-rename of its input:
  identical AST, identical literals, identifiers in bijection with their bindings.
- On-device JavaScript is 112,939 bytes after the collision-cache, sprite-placement and
  Crater Motel music updates.
- Exit now follows the OS guide path: request the mode/menu transition, then let
  Pip.changeMenu() call Big Iron's remove() cleanup. Direct Pip.CURRENT.fullscreen
  writes were removed; fullscreen is declared only in the returned app object.
- Big Iron no longer calls Pip.startTimers() or Pip.setWatches(); the OS owns those
  timers. This prevents stacked flip/selector intervals after exiting the holotape.
- Exit no longer saves the active module. World state is saved during play and LOAD GAME
  checks SAV.JSON on disk instead of trusting leftover in-memory state.
- World motorcycle left/right sprite lookup is swapped for riding only, fixing reversed
  bike art without changing walking sprites.
- Shop video lookup restored through existing battle clips. Raider and Solomon
  fights stay video-driven; Solomon's office fight now uses a faster sprite battle
  scene with Chuck fighting beside the player.
- Motorcycle sprites and Chuck's heart popup now clear their full draw area while moving.
- Crater Motel now plays the LSTAR chiptune.
- Entering Rot's Crossing from Sunscar now lands on the right-side road.
- Race bikes are mirrored to face the track direction, and the track surface now reads as
  packed Big Iron road instead of a clean highway.
- Rotjaw's race bike and trackside motorcycle sprites were redrawn so he reads as Rotjaw
  on a bike instead of overlapping scenery.
- Motorcycle road exits now pass through automatically while riding, preserving the bike
  across Sunscar, Rot's Crossing and Dogtown road transitions.

0.58.0 (2026-08-15)
- Rot's Crossing rebuilt in the Sunscar art style; the stretched right-edge artifact is gone.
- The race track is now graded dirt with a packed berm, worn racing ruts and a start/finish
  stripe, instead of a two-pixel outline drawn on the grass.
- Collision for Rot's Crossing regenerated from the new artwork and verified reachable to
  every interaction point and spawn.
- Battle videos repaired: player-side clips were routed at a VIDEO/Granny folder that no
  longer ships, so attacks, faints, victory and defeat silently never played.
- Pause STAT tab reads the live Pip-Boy record instead of a profile argument no caller passed.
- Manifest regenerated from the folder: 12 phantom video entries removed, 37 real assets
  added, including every hotel room background and all motorcycle sprites.
- Modules renamed from single letters to descriptive names; dead C.JS and R.JS removed.
- Rotjaw now uses the bike sprite added in 0.57.0 but never read.
- A finished playthrough's SAV.JSON was shipping inside the tape and is removed.

0.57.0 (2026-08-15)
- Big Iron Hotel main lobby became a hub: Kitchen, Laundry, Boiler Room, Ammo Workshop.
- Penthouse opens after six Scorch Rounds. Motorcycle sprites with the dog in the sidecar.

